Continue from the completed GRUB/ISO boot-chain repair.

TASK: Complete Rebuntu Milestone M1 — Bootable Live System.

Repository:
  /home/bvrznski/rebuntu

Builder:
  rebuntu-builder
  /work/rebuntu
  rootfs: /work/rootfs

Do not touch unrelated active src/ implementation.

GOAL

Make the complete boot path work reliably:

  firmware
    -> GRUB
    -> Linux kernel
    -> initramfs
    -> Plymouth
    -> casper
    -> systemd
    -> Rebuntu live userspace

This task is not complete merely because GRUB loads the kernel.

REQUIREMENTS

1. Boot the generated ISO under QEMU and follow the boot until live userspace is reached.

2. Diagnose and repair every failure encountered after GRUB:
   - kernel command line
   - initramfs
   - casper
   - squashfs discovery/mount
   - overlay filesystem
   - systemd
   - device management
   - networking prerequisites
   - live-user creation/login
   - TTY availability

3. Preserve:
   boot=casper quiet splash ---

4. Preserve working BIOS and UEFI boot behavior.

5. Preserve the Rebuntu Plymouth initramfs integration:
   FRAMEBUFFER=y
   rebuntu.plymouth
   rebuntu.script
   script.so

6. Do not hide boot failures with quiet/splash.
   For diagnostics, temporarily use verbose/debug kernel parameters where useful,
   but restore production parameters afterward.

7. Add a deterministic boot smoke test.

The automated test should prove that the machine progresses beyond GRUB and reaches
a recognizable live-userspace milestone.

Prefer a serial-console marker or systemd target over screenshot-based detection.

8. Add bounded timeouts and useful diagnostics.

9. Ensure the generated live system identifies itself as Rebuntu where appropriate.

10. Do not modify the host bootloader/initramfs/Plymouth.

ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

- BIOS ISO boot reaches live userspace.
- UEFI ISO boot reaches live userspace where the environment supports OVMF.
- /casper filesystem is successfully mounted.
- systemd starts.
- a usable TTY/live session is reachable.
- boot does not drop to initramfs emergency shell.
- automated smoke test detects failure before userspace.
- existing ISO structural tests continue to pass.
- Plymouth remains integrated.
- all changes are committed as reproducible source/build logic.

Report:
- failures discovered
- root causes
- files changed
- tests added
- BIOS result
- UEFI result
- final boot timeline
- remaining limitations

Continue from the working Rebuntu M1 live boot.

TASK: Turn the Rebuntu Plymouth implementation into a production-quality boot experience.

Do not redesign the boot pipeline unless required to fix a demonstrated problem.

VISUAL TARGET

Near-black background.

Centered canonical Rebuntu logo:
- monochrome white
- three curved/tapered blade or petal forms
- 120-degree rotational composition
- negative-space channels between forms
- central aperture MUST remain visible
- compact triangular/triskelion silhouette
- sharp outward tips

Below it:

  R E B U N T U

Minimal typography.

Below the wordmark:
- very thin progress bar
- approximately 2–3 physical pixels at 4K
- approximately 35–45% of wordmark width
- cyan -> blue -> violet progression
- no percentage
- no "Loading"
- no spinner
- no status text

BEHAVIOR

Progress must reflect Plymouth/system boot progress rather than an independent fake timer where the Plymouth API permits it.

At completion:
- briefly reach 100%
- clean transition/fade into live userspace
- avoid console flashes where reasonably possible

IMPLEMENTATION REQUIREMENTS

1. Audit the current rebuntu.script rather than assuming it is valid.
2. Validate it against the Plymouth script plugin available on Ubuntu Jammy.
3. Replace the procedural placeholder logo with the canonical Rebuntu asset if the repository contains one.
4. If no canonical asset exists, DO NOT invent a new final logo. Preserve a clearly identified temporary placeholder and report the missing asset.
5. Make layout resolution-independent.
6. Correctly handle common aspect ratios and HiDPI/4K.
7. Keep the theme packaged by rebuntu-plymouth-theme.
8. Ensure the final theme is embedded in initramfs.
9. Add static validation/tests where possible.
10. Boot-test it in QEMU.

Do not modify host Plymouth.

ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

- theme loads without Plymouth script/parser errors
- logo/wordmark/progress layout is centered and scalable
- progress reacts to actual Plymouth progress callbacks
- no Ubuntu branding appears
- no spinner/status/percentage appears
- transition to userspace is clean
- initramfs contains the production theme
- boot still passes M1 smoke tests

Report implementation and any remaining visual limitations.

Continue from the completed M1 bootable Rebuntu live system.

TASK: Complete Rebuntu Milestone M2 — Usable Live Environment.

GOAL

After selecting "Try Rebuntu", provide a coherent usable live operating system,
not merely a booted kernel/root filesystem.

Implement and integrate:

1. Live user lifecycle
   - deterministic live user
   - sane groups
   - sudo policy appropriate for live media
   - correct home directory
   - runtime directories
   - shell environment

2. System identity
   - Rebuntu hostname
   - /etc/os-release
   - machine-readable distro identity
   - issue/motd where appropriate
   - remove inappropriate visible Ubuntu branding while preserving truthful technical ancestry where required

3. Networking
   - NetworkManager
   - DHCP
   - DNS
   - loopback
   - predictable live networking behavior

4. Console usability
   - functional TTY
   - login/session
   - terminal basics
   - correct locale fallback
   - UTF-8
   - eliminate current avoidable locale warnings

5. Essential tools
   Provide a deliberately selected minimal troubleshooting/admin toolset.
   Do not turn the live image into an uncontrolled package dump.

6. systemd
   - sane default target
   - no avoidable failed units
   - clean shutdown/reboot
   - appropriate live-media services

7. Persistence boundaries
   Clearly separate immutable SquashFS content from writable live overlay state.

8. Tests
   Extend automated QEMU smoke tests to verify:
   - userspace reached
   - live user exists
   - system identifies as Rebuntu
   - networking stack starts
   - writable overlay works
   - systemd is operational
   - shutdown/reboot works

9. Reproducibility
   Everything must originate from repository-controlled packages/configuration/scripts.

Do not manually patch generated rootfs state as the final solution.

ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

A user can boot "Try Rebuntu", reach a usable live environment, use a shell,
inspect the system, use networking, create files in the writable live layer,
and cleanly reboot/shutdown.

All M1 tests must remain green.

Continue from the working M2 Rebuntu live environment.

TASK: Implement the first graphical Rebuntu live session.

GOAL

Boot:

  GRUB
   -> Plymouth
   -> systemd
   -> graphical target
   -> Rebuntu graphical live session

Do not blindly install ubuntu-desktop.

First audit the repository for existing Rebuntu GUI/session/window-manager/
compositor/display-manager decisions and reuse them where appropriate.

REQUIREMENTS

- minimal deliberate graphical stack
- display manager or direct session startup, whichever matches Rebuntu architecture
- X11/Wayland choice must be explicit and justified
- keyboard/mouse
- correct resolution
- virtio/QEMU graphics
- terminal
- basic file access
- NetworkManager integration
- session logout
- shutdown/reboot
- live user integration

VISUAL BASELINE

Establish the initial Rebuntu visual system:
- graphite / near-black
- restrained retro-tech influence
- clean rather than novelty-retro
- consistent monospace use where appropriate
- Rebuntu identity
- no accidental Ubuntu wallpaper/branding

Do not spend this pass building the final visual theme.
Build the architecture and baseline first.

Add automated tests proving that graphical.target/session startup succeeds.

Preserve headless/TTY recovery if the graphical stack fails.

ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

"Try Rebuntu" reaches a usable graphical live session while retaining working
TTY and recovery paths.

Continue from the working graphical/live Rebuntu environment.

TASK: Complete Rebuntu Milestone M3 — Installable Rebuntu.

GOAL

Support the complete lifecycle:

  boot ISO
    -> Try/Install Rebuntu
    -> installer
    -> target disk
    -> bootloader installation
    -> reboot
    -> remove ISO
    -> installed Rebuntu boots independently

REQUIREMENTS

1. Audit available installer approaches before choosing one.
2. Integrate the chosen installer into Rebuntu packaging/build architecture.
3. Do not create a fragile one-off disk-copy script and call it an installer.

Support at minimum:
- GPT
- UEFI installation
- BIOS installation where practical
- EFI System Partition
- root filesystem
- swap strategy
- hostname
- user creation
- timezone
- locale
- keyboard layout
- bootloader
- installed Rebuntu package set

Installer must distinguish live-only configuration from installed-system configuration.

The installed system must:
- identify as Rebuntu
- boot without ISO
- have working networking
- have working user login
- have working graphical session if M2 graphical environment is present
- have correct GRUB configuration
- have correct initramfs/Plymouth configuration

SAFETY

Automated installer tests MUST operate only on explicitly created disposable virtual disks.

Never select or modify arbitrary host block devices.

TESTING

Create an automated VM lifecycle test:

  create disposable virtual disk
       ↓
  boot Rebuntu ISO
       ↓
  install
       ↓
  shutdown
       ↓
  detach ISO
       ↓
  boot virtual disk
       ↓
  verify installed Rebuntu userspace

Where possible test UEFI and BIOS independently.

ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

A fresh VM can be installed from the Rebuntu ISO and subsequently boot Rebuntu
from its virtual disk with the ISO removed.

M1/M2 tests must remain green.

TASK: Harden Rebuntu from a working Ubuntu-derived custom ISO into a maintainable derivative distribution.

Do not redesign unrelated src/.

Audit and normalize:

- package ownership of Rebuntu files
- rebuntu-base and other metapackages
- Debian package metadata
- dependencies
- conffiles
- maintainer scripts
- alternatives
- triggers
- repository/local
- APT source configuration
- package versioning
- release/version identity
- upgrade paths
- image manifests
- build reproducibility
- generated-vs-source boundaries

Remove cases where build scripts directly mutate rootfs files that should instead
be owned by a Rebuntu package.

Prefer:

  source
    -> Debian package
    -> repository
    -> apt/dpkg
    -> rootfs

over:

  build script
    -> arbitrary cp/sed into rootfs

Audit rebuntu-plymouth-theme specifically and convert it into a clean,
maintainable Debian package if it is still using ad-hoc package assembly.

Add package ownership and upgrade tests.

GOAL

After this pass, Rebuntu should have a clean distribution-engineering foundation
for continued development rather than accumulating image-builder hacks.

DON'T MANUALLY EDIT PNG'S
